a constant integer holds its encoding first

The layouts were read out of a binary that has these instances rather than inferred from a fault.
iTerm2 carries 459 constant arrays, 42 constant dictionaries and 907 constant integers in
__DATA_CONST, and the bind records give every address.

  ARRAY        stride 0x18   isa, count, objects              already right
  DICTIONARY   stride 0x28   isa, options, count, keys, objs  was wrong by one field
  INTEGER      stride 0x18   isa, encoding, value             had the two swapped

word1 of the dictionary is 1 in all 42 instances so it is not the count, and word2 satisfies
objects - keys == word2 * 8 in 42 of 42. The integer's word1 is a pointer into __TEXT shared
between instances of the same type, and word2 is the value.

--- a/src/NSConstantNumbers.m
+++ b/src/NSConstantNumbers.m
@@ -26,10 +26,24 @@
  * it wants and the class converts: iTerm2 asked a constant integer for shortValue and the missing
  * accessor was an unrecognized selector that terminated the application on its main nib. */
 
+/*
+ * THE ENCODING COMES FIRST, READ OUT OF A BINARY rather than assumed.
+ *
+ * iTerm2 carries 907 of these in __DATA_CONST,__objc_intobj, 24 bytes apart, and the words say
+ * which field is which:
+ *
+ *     0x101554190: isa=0  w1=0x101024b1d (a pointer into __TEXT)  w2=0x32
+ *     0x1015541a8: isa=0  w1=0x101024b1d                          w2=0x19
+ *     0x1015541c0: isa=0  w1=0x10102861d                          w2=0x10000
+ *
+ * w1 is the type encoding string, shared between instances of the same type, and w2 is the value.
+ * Declared the other way round, every constant integer read its value as a string pointer: a
+ * constant 1 became a pointer to address 1, and iTerm2 died in -[NSNumber hash] dereferencing it.
+ */
 @interface NSConstantIntegerNumber : NSNumber {
 @public
-    long long _value;
     const char *_encoding;
+    long long _value;
 }
 @end
 
